@model List<algo.ViewModels.AlgorithmReportViewModel>


@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Sorting Algorithms Detailed Report";
}
@section Styles{
    <style>

        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
        }

        .page {
            width: 21cm; 
            height: 29.7cm; 
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            justify-content: flex-start;
            align-items: center; 
            padding: 20mm; 
            box-sizing: border-box;
            page-break-after: always;
        }

        .title {
            text-align: center;
            font-size: 24px;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        .section {
            margin-bottom: 15mm; 
            width: 100%;
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            align-items: flex-start;
        }

        .section-title {
            font-size: 18px;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            text-align: left;
        }

        .content {
            width: 100%;
            text-align: justify;
            font-size: 12pt;
        }

        ul {
            padding-left: 20px;
        }

        .table-responsive {
            width: 100%;
            margin: 0 auto;
        }

        .table {
            width: 100%;
            text-align: center;
        }
    </style>
}


<div class="page" id="page-1">
    <div class="title">Algorithm Benchmark Project</div>
    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Student Information</div>
        <div class="content">
            <p><strong>İbrahim Furkan Akbulut</strong> - 202010020034</p>
            <p><strong>İsmail Serin</strong> - 202010020102</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Project Overview</div>
        <div class="content">
            <p>The Algorithm Benchmark Project is designed to analyze the performance of various sorting algorithms, including Heap Sort, Radix Sort, Shell Sort, and Quick Sort. The analysis evaluates CPU usage, RAM usage, and execution time for different array sizes and types.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="page" id="page-2">
    <div class="title">Algorithm Analysis</div>
    
    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Test Environment Specifications</div>
        <div class="content">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Processor:</strong> Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-7700HQ CPU  2.80GHz</li>
                <li><strong>RAM:</strong> 16.0 GB (15.9 GB available)  2400 MHz</li>
                <li><strong>System Type:</strong> 64-bit Operating System, x64-based processor</li>
                <li><strong>Operating System:</strong> Windows 10</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Algorithms Overview</div>
        <div class="content">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Quick Sort:</strong> A divide-and-conquer algorithm with average time complexity of O(n log n). Suitable for most datasets but performs poorly with already sorted data in its naive implementation.</li>
                <li><strong>Heap Sort:</strong> A comparison-based sorting algorithm with O(n log n) time complexity. Uses a binary heap structure and is efficient for large datasets.</li>
                <li><strong>Shell Sort:</strong> An in-place comparison sort that improves upon insertion sort. Its performance depends on the gap sequence used.</li>
                <li><strong>Merge Sort:</strong> A stable sorting algorithm with O(n log n) complexity. Suitable for large datasets but requires additional memory.</li>
                <li><strong>Radix Sort:</strong> A non-comparative sorting algorithm that processes digits individually. It performs well with integer or fixed-width string keys.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Dataset Characteristics</div>
        <div class="content">
            <p>Datasets used for analysis include:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Randomly ordered data.</li>
                <li>Partially sorted data.</li>
                <li>Reverse ordered data.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Data sizes tested:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Small:</strong> 1,000 elements.</li>
                <li><strong>Medium:</strong> 10,000 elements.</li>
                <li><strong>Large:</strong> 100,000 elements.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="page" id="page-3">
    <div class="title">Theoretical and Experimental Results</div>
    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Performance Expectations</div>
        <div class="content">
            <p>Theoretical analysis indicates the following performance patterns:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Quick Sort:</strong> Best with random datasets; struggles with reverse-ordered data without optimizations.</li>
                <li><strong>Heap Sort:</strong> Consistent performance across all datasets.</li>
                <li><strong>Shell Sort:</strong> Highly dependent on gap sequence; not as efficient as others for large datasets.</li>
                <li><strong>Merge Sort:</strong> Performs well across all datasets but requires significant memory.</li>
                <li><strong>Radix Sort:</strong> Excels with integer datasets and large sizes due to non-comparative nature.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
   
</div>
