<div class="page" id="page-9">
    <div class="title">Theoretical vs Experimental Analysis</div>

    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Comparison Overview</div>
        <div class="content">
            <p>This analysis compares the theoretical expectations with experimental results across different array sizes and sorting conditions. The comparison focuses on time complexity manifestation in real-world scenarios.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Time Complexity Analysis</div>
        <div class="content">
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Algorithm</th>
                            <th>Theoretical Complexity</th>
                            <th>Experimental Observation</th>
                            <th>Correlation</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Quick Sort</td>
                            <td>O(n log n) average<br>O(n²) worst case</td>
                            <td>
                                - Excellent performance on random data (1K: 6.598ms, 10K: 8.302ms)<br>
                                - Significant degradation on reverse sorted (100K: 23166.589ms)
                            </td>
                            <td>Strong correlation with theory - quadratic growth observed in worst case scenarios</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Heap Sort</td>
                            <td>O(n log n) all cases</td>
                            <td>
                                - Consistent performance across all data types<br>
                                - Random 1K: 6.168ms, 10K: 8.963ms, 100K: 42.921ms
                            </td>
                            <td>Perfect match with theoretical predictions - logarithmic growth confirmed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Shell Sort</td>
                            <td>O(n log² n) to O(n²)</td>
                            <td>
                                - Variable performance based on data ordering<br>
                                - Best case close to O(n log n)
                            </td>
                            <td>Experimental results better than worst-case theoretical bounds</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Merge Sort</td>
                            <td>O(n log n) all cases</td>
                            <td>
                                - Stable performance across data types<br>
                                - Higher memory usage observed
                            </td>
                            <td>Consistent with theoretical predictions, with memory overhead as expected</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Radix Sort</td>
                            <td>O(nk) where k is digit count</td>
                            <td>
                                - Linear-like scaling observed<br>
                                - Efficient for large datasets
                            </td>
                            <td>Performs according to theoretical expectations for integer data</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <div class="section-title">Key Findings</div>
        <div class="content">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Quick Sort:</strong> Theoretical predictions of O(n²) worst-case confirmed in reverse-sorted arrays, with execution time increasing dramatically at 100K elements.</li>
                <li><strong>Heap Sort:</strong> Most consistent performer, maintaining O(n log n) complexity across all scenarios as theoretically expected.</li>
                <li><strong>Memory Usage:</strong> Experimental results show higher memory consumption for Merge Sort, aligning with theoretical space complexity predictions.</li>
                <li><strong>CPU Utilization:</strong> Varies between 40-70% for most cases, with spikes during worst-case scenarios.</li>
                <li><strong>Scaling Behavior:</strong> All algorithms show expected scaling patterns when moving from 1K to 100K elements, validating theoretical growth rates.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
